<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  what a run shows while it is not ready yet, drawn over the surface the guest will render into.

  **it is a screen rather than a widget, and that is the whole redesign.** a bar floating on the raw
  surface reads as something that has gone wrong on a black screen; a card on a filled background
  reads as the app, still working. it also solves a real problem the floating version had: the
  surface behind it is black until the guest draws, so text coloured for a light scheme was dark text
  on black. nothing here sits on the surface - the background is drawn.

  **and it is drawn black rather than in the scheme's own surface colour, which is the one place this
  screen does not follow the theme.** what is behind this view is the surface a guest is about to
  render into, which is black until it does, and what replaces this screen is a game's first frame,
  which is very often black as well. a field in the scheme's colour therefore steps to black at the
  exact moment the picture appears - the screen going away reads as a flash rather than as a card
  being lifted off. black on both sides of that moment means only the card disappears.

  the card is still the scheme's, which is what keeps this the app's screen rather than a blank one:
  the palette is carried by the thing being read, and the field around it is the dark the guest is
  about to fill. on this panel an unlit pixel costs nothing, which is the same reason the Dark theme
  is black rather than merely dark.

  **the card is the game's cover with a column of text beside it, and the cover runs to the card's
  own edges.** artwork inset inside a card, at the size of a line of type, reads as a row in a list;
  artwork that *is* one end of the card reads as a piece of the design, and it is what gives the card
  a reason to be wide rather than merely being wide. the shape at that end is the card's own - see
  ShapeAppearance.SharpDroid.LoadingArt, which rounds the two corners it shares with the card and
  squares the two in the middle.

  **the card is filled with the container role rather than the dialog's, and that is a change of what
  this is.** it began as a dialog's clothes on a card, because a dialog is a window and this
  activity's window is the surface a guest renders into. with the artwork at its edge it is no longer
  anything like a popup, so it takes colorSurfaceContainer - which every scheme here names, unlike the
  dialog role above it, and which is therefore the scheme's own colour in all five rather than the
  library's baseline in one.

  **its height is fixed and its width is capped.** a height that follows its contents grows a line at
  the moment the bar gains a figure, partway through the wait and with nothing to explain it. the
  percentage keeps its place whether or not there is a number in it, so the card is the same size for
  the whole of a launch and between one launch and the next.

  ConstraintLayout for exactly one property: `layout_constraintWidth_max`. the card wants to be a
  readable column rather than the width of a landscape panel, and a fixed width would be wrong on a
  device of another shape - no other container here can say "as wide as there is room for, up to
  this".

  there is no `-land` variant and there is not meant to be. the activity is pinned landscape - see
  the manifest - so a second composition would be one nothing can reach.
-->
<androidx.constraintlayout.widget.ConstraintLayout
    xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent"
    android:background="@android:color/black">

    <!-- **160dp, and the artwork is a 160dp square inside it.** icon0.png is square, so a panel of
         any other proportion crops it - and a cover whose art reaches its own edges loses that art
         rather than losing margin. tying the panel's width to the card's height is what makes the
         crop impossible rather than merely unlikely.

         **it is a minimum rather than a fixed height, and the reason the height was pinned is
         untouched by that.** what a fixed height prevents is the card growing a line partway through
         a wait, when the bar gains a figure - and every view that can appear during a boot still fits
         inside 160dp, so the card is the same size for the whole of one and between one launch and
         the next. a refusal is the only thing that makes it taller, and a refusal is terminal: there
         is no boot left underneath for a resize to disturb. -->
    <com.google.android.material.card.MaterialCardView
        android:layout_width="0dp"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:minHeight="160dp"
        android:layout_marginHorizontal="24dp"
        app:cardBackgroundColor="?attr/colorSurfaceContainer"
        app:cardElevation="0dp"
        app:layout_constraintBottom_toBottomOf="parent"
        app:layout_constraintEnd_toEndOf="parent"
        app:layout_constraintStart_toStartOf="parent"
        app:layout_constraintTop_toTopOf="parent"
        app:layout_constraintWidth_max="560dp"
        app:shapeAppearance="?attr/shapeAppearanceCornerExtraLarge"
        app:strokeWidth="0dp">

        <LinearLayout
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="match_parent"
            android:orientation="horizontal">

            <!-- **its own shape rather than the card's clipping**, so this does not depend on how a
                 card treats a child that reaches its corners. it is GONE rather than a placeholder
                 when a launch named no artwork, because `am start` names none - and an empty grey
                 panel of this size reads as a cover that failed to load rather than as one nobody
                 sent. the text column then simply takes the whole card. -->
            <com.google.android.material.imageview.ShapeableImageView
                android:id="@+id/loading_cover"
                android:layout_width="160dp"
                android:layout_height="match_parent"
                android:importantForAccessibility="no"
                android:scaleType="centerCrop"
                android:visibility="gone"
                app:shapeAppearanceOverlay="@style/ShapeAppearance.SharpDroid.LoadingArt"
                tools:src="@drawable/ic_game_placeholder"
                tools:visibility="visible" />

            <!-- **centred in the card's height rather than padded to it**, which is what lets the
                 height be a fixed number without anything having to add up to it: the block is
                 whatever it is and the air divides itself. 24dp at the sides is the dialog padding
                 this card kept when it stopped being a dialog in every other respect. -->
            <LinearLayout
                android:layout_width="0dp"
                android:layout_height="match_parent"
                android:layout_weight="1"
                android:gravity="center_vertical"
                android:orientation="vertical"
                android:paddingHorizontal="24dp">

                <!-- two lines and then ellipsized, not the marquee the game list's cards use. a card
                     in a grid is one line tall and every card has to be the same height, so a long
                     name there has nowhere to go but sideways; here there is room for a second line,
                     and a name sliding under a phase line that is also changing would be two moving
                     things in a block of four. -->
                <TextView
                    android:id="@+id/loading_title"
                    android:layout_width="match_parent"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:ellipsize="end"
                    android:maxLines="2"
                    android:textAppearance="?attr/textAppearanceTitleLarge"
                    android:textColor="?attr/colorOnSurface"
                    tools:text="Dreaming Sarah" />

                <!-- what is happening to it right now. **it is one line and it is held to one**,
                     because every phase name is short and a line that reflowed to two would move the
                     bar under it a dozen times in a boot.

                     **the gap under this line is where the card's two halves are set apart**, and the
                     two numbers that do it are not independent. the block is centred in a fixed
                     height, so widening the gap by 10dp moves the name and the phase up by 5 and the
                     bar and its figure down by 5; the 2dp under the figure adds 2dp of block, which
                     moves the whole thing up by 1 more. the pair land at 6dp up and 4dp down, which
                     is the asymmetry the composition wants - the name is the thing read first and it
                     sits a little above centre. change either number and recompute the other. -->
                <TextView
                    android:id="@+id/loading_detail"
                    android:layout_width="match_parent"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_marginTop="4dp"
                    android:ellipsize="end"
                    android:singleLine="true"
                    android:textAppearance="?attr/textAppearanceBodyMedium"
                    android:textColor="?attr/colorOnSurfaceVariant"
                    tools:text="Preparing the display…" />

                <!-- **the track is the card's own outline colour**, which is what keeps an empty bar
                     reading as a groove in the card rather than as a second surface on it. every
                     scheme names that role, which the surfaceContainer* family above outlineVariant
                     does not - an unnamed role is Material3's baseline lilac, and this app is three
                     quarters monochrome.

                     **the range is a thousand rather than a hundred**, and that is not precision for
                     its own sake: the boot bar is redrawn on every animation frame from a continuous
                     estimate, so a hundred steps over seven seconds is a visible tick every 70 ms.
                     the figure below is still a whole percent - the resolution is the sweep's, not
                     the label's.

                     **the stop indicator is off, and it is a feature rather than an accident.**
                     Material draws a dot at the end of the track, in the indicator's own colour, to
                     mark where a determinate bar is heading. on a bar that sweeps continuously for
                     several seconds it reads instead as the leading edge of the line, detached and
                     already arrived - and it marks a target that is already stated twice over, by
                     the figure under the bar and by the track simply ending. it is also drawn in
                     determinate mode alone, so leaving it on would make this screen structurally
                     different with the estimate on and off for a reason that has nothing to do with
                     the estimate. -->
                <com.google.android.material.progressindicator.LinearProgressIndicator
                    android:id="@+id/loading_bar"
                    android:layout_width="match_parent"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_marginTop="28dp"
                    android:max="1000"
                    app:indicatorColor="?attr/colorPrimary"
                    app:trackColor="?attr/colorOutlineVariant"
                    app:trackCornerRadius="4dp"
                    app:trackStopIndicatorSize="0dp"
                    app:trackThickness="8dp" />

                <!-- **under the bar and at its end, which is where the bar is going.** over it, the
                     figure competes with the heading; at the start, the eye leaves it before the bar
                     has said anything.

                     **it is invisible rather than gone when there is no figure to give**, which is
                     the opposite of what it was and follows from the card's height being fixed. a
                     view that is gone closes the space it occupied, so an indeterminate launch would
                     centre a shorter block in the same card and put every line somewhere else. -->
                <!-- **what a launch says when it is not going to happen**, and the two of them
                     replace the bar and its figure rather than joining them: there is no progress
                     to report once a run has given up, and a bar left on the card would say there
                     still is.

                     both are gone until there is something to refuse, so an ordinary boot lays out
                     exactly the block it always did. the reason wraps - it is a sentence rather
                     than a phase name, and it is the one thing on this card somebody has to read
                     rather than glance at.

                     the button ends the run, which is what takes this process with it. it is the
                     only way out of a refused launch, so it is the only thing on the card that can
                     be pressed. -->
                <TextView
                    android:id="@+id/loading_reason"
                    android:layout_width="match_parent"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_marginTop="8dp"
                    android:textAppearance="?attr/textAppearanceBodyMedium"
                    android:textColor="?attr/colorOnSurfaceVariant"
                    android:visibility="gone"
                    tools:text="Could not find Dreaming Sarah. It may have been moved or deleted."
                    tools:visibility="visible" />

                <com.google.android.material.button.MaterialButton
                    android:id="@+id/loading_close"
                    style="@style/Widget.Material3.Button.TextButton"
                    android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_gravity="end"
                    android:layout_marginTop="8dp"
                    android:text="@string/launch_close"
                    android:visibility="gone"
                    tools:visibility="visible" />

                <TextView
                    android:id="@+id/loading_percent"
                    android:layout_width="match_parent"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_marginBottom="2dp"
                    android:layout_marginTop="6dp"
                    android:gravity="end"
                    android:textAppearance="?attr/textAppearanceLabelLarge"
                    android:textColor="?attr/colorOnSurfaceVariant"
                    android:visibility="invisible"
                    tools:text="38%" />

            </LinearLayout>

        </LinearLayout>

    </com.google.android.material.card.MaterialCardView>

</androidx.constraintlayout.widget.ConstraintLayout>
